1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,400
[Mrs. Lan, in Vietnamese] “Hello, this is Lan on Hawthorne Street.
My power has been out for two days…”

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:06,591
That's Mrs. Lan. Power out
two days since the ice storm.

3
00:00:06,591 --> 00:00:11,117
She doesn't read English. She just left a
voice note for her neighborhood hotline.

4
00:00:11,117 --> 00:00:12,317
Watch the clock.

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:16,116
She heard it read back in
Vietnamese and said yes.

6
00:00:16,116 --> 00:00:21,830
The group's own rules, not the AI, set
the amount: a hundred and fifty dollars.

7
00:00:21,830 --> 00:00:24,000
Two organizers, on two phones.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,782
Paid, into her Venmo,
with a message in Vietnamese.

9
00:00:34,782 --> 00:00:38,600
Twenty-two point three seconds from
voice note to the SUCCESS webhook,

10
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:42,691
on our PayPal stand-in. Our organizers
tapped on cue; with real volunteers,

11
00:00:42,691 --> 00:00:45,800
it's as long as two neighbors
take to check their phones.

12
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:49,244
This is Neighborline:
a hotline, not a dashboard.

13
00:00:49,244 --> 00:00:53,751
After the Eaton fire, one LA mutual-aid
network took three hundred requests

14
00:00:53,751 --> 00:00:58,378
overnight, in Instagram comments. Most
groups still run on spreadsheets and a

15
00:00:58,378 --> 00:01:00,000
volunteer's personal Venmo.

16
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Rosa asks for groceries, in Spanish.
Same rules, two approvals.

17
00:01:12,426 --> 00:01:17,626
Her email has no PayPal account yet,
so PayPal reports the payout unclaimed.

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,621
Most systems stop there.
Neighborline messages her, in Spanish,

19
00:01:21,621 --> 00:01:23,000
exactly how to claim it.

20
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:24,700
She does. Success.

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,600
Someone posing as the organizers,
with a Venmo handle one letter off?

22
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:36,000
Approve is disabled on
every phone. No money moves.

23
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:41,157
Under the hood: the agents that talk to
neighbors have no PayPal tools at all.

24
00:01:41,157 --> 00:01:44,894
One separate approval service
holds the Payouts credentials,

25
00:01:44,894 --> 00:01:50,000
and it pays only on a record signed with
the passkeys of two different organizers.

26
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:54,149
Every AI output is checked
against a schema, repaired once,

27
00:01:54,149 --> 00:01:57,150
or the neighbor is asked
again, never guessed.

28
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,784
The batch ID is a hash of that approval,
so a double tap can't pay twice.

29
00:02:02,784 --> 00:02:07,895
Webhooks are signature-verified, and
every payout is reconciled against PayPal

30
00:02:07,895 --> 00:02:09,140
Transaction Search.

31
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:17,879
Donors give with PayPal or Venmo. The
public report is AI-written around numbers

32
00:02:17,879 --> 00:02:22,272
that code pulls from the ledger,
and it never names a neighbor.

33
00:02:22,272 --> 00:02:27,851
Built for the roughly nine hundred volunteer groups that mobilize when the storm

34
00:02:27,851 --> 00:02:29,051
hits.

35
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,742
Neighborline is open source. Try the hotline yourself, no login, at the link below.

36
00:02:35,742 --> 00:02:41,000
Because the morning after a storm,
help should sound like your own language.
